<%# locals: (playthrough:, command: nil, error: nil, safety_notice: false, refusal: nil, autofocus: false) %>
<%# EVERYTHING A TURN CHANGES, in one Turbo Stream target.

    The page and the job render this partial in the same two states:

      PlaythroughsController#show -- idle, a plain page load or a Resume link
      NarrationJob             -- streaming, then idle when the turn finishes

    The command's HTTP response carries no replacement. The job's pending and
    final pages arrive over the same channel, so a fast turn cannot be erased
    by a late pending response.

    So the end of a turn is a `replace` of this one element rather than a page
    reload, and the log, the location line and the form all arrive together.
    Nothing outside `#turn_log` moves -- in particular `turbo_stream_from` and
    `#bottom` stay put, so the subscription is not torn down and rebuilt on
    every turn.

    `command` is the string the player just typed while a turn is in flight, and
    nil the rest of the time. It is the whole of the difference between the two
    states.

    `autofocus` is true only on a real page load, and that is not fussiness.
    Turbo's stream renderer focuses the first `[autofocus]` element it finds in a
    broadcast -- with a plain `.focus()`, no `preventScroll` -- so shipping the
    attribute over the cable yanks the viewport to the foot of the log at the end
    of every turn, throwing away exactly the scroll position this whole change
    exists to keep. Measured in a browser; it is not theoretical. `play.js` puts
    focus back after a turn itself, with `preventScroll`. %>
<div id="turn_log">
  <%# A turn that failed -- a model that would not answer, a schema it ignored,
      a character sheet cut off at its cap. The persisted state is shown and
      the player gets the input back along with a line saying the turn did
      not finish; the old SSE `error` event only stopped the cursor blinking and
      left no way on but a reload.

      The copy is the APP'S (`Playthrough::TurnFailureNotice`), not an
      exception's `message`: every reason a turn fails is internal, and one of
      them used to print a `max_length` cap and a fragment of model output the
      app had already decided not to keep. `NarrationJob` logs the real error.

      ONE THING THAT ARRIVES HERE IS NOT A FAILED TURN: an install with no model
      configured, whose turn finished on the engine's own factual prose. It is
      the one reason that is not internal, so it is the one that names what to
      do -- `Playthrough::SetupNotice`. %>
  <% if error %>
    <p class="alert"><%= error %></p>
  <% end %>

  <% if command.nil? && (unfinished = Playthrough::Command.resume_target(playthrough)) %>
    <div class="notice" data-saved-turn>
      <p>Your command is saved but has not finished: <q><%= unfinished.command %></q>.</p>
      <% if unfinished.status == "running" && !unfinished.recoverable? %>
        <p>This older turn cannot be resumed safely. You can keep its saved effects and continue; the unfinished action will not be replayed.</p>
        <% if unfinished.journal.blank? %>
          <%= button_to "Keep saved state and continue", acknowledge_interruption_playthrough_turns_path(playthrough),
                        params: { command_id: unfinished.id } %>
        <% end %>
      <% else %>
        <%= button_to "Resume saved turn", playthrough_turns_path(playthrough),
                      params: { command: unfinished.command, request_token: unfinished.request_token } %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# One wrapper, so the stylesheet can dim every logged turn but the last one.
      `streaming` says the newest turn is not in the log at all -- it is the
      #stream div below, which is what the player is actually reading -- so no
      logged turn should claim full-strength colour while it fills in. %>
  <%# `verdicts` is resolved once for the whole log rather than per entry -- the
      log is the entire playthrough, and the turn partial asks about every turn
      in it. Read here rather than in the partial so every caller of this
      partial pay for it once -- and empty when the instrument is off, so a game
      nobody is evaluating pays nothing for it. %>
  <% verdicts = Playthrough::Debug.enabled? ? playthrough.feedback_by_scene : {} %>
  <div class="<%= class_names("log", streaming: command) %>">
    <%= render partial: "turns/turn", collection: playthrough.turn_log, as: :turn,
               locals: { playthrough: playthrough, verdicts: verdicts } %>
  </div>

  <%# OUTSIDE THE FICTION, AND WHERE THE TURN WOULD HAVE BEEN. `BaseAgent`
      suppressed a response that answered this turn with a real-world crisis
      line, so no scene was written and this stands in its place -- in the app's
      own voice, at the foot of the log where `#stream` was and where the player
      is already looking. NOT above the log with the error, which is fine for a
      one-line reason and would put this off the top of the screen on a long
      transcript: the page anchors at `#bottom`.

      Outside `.log` on purpose too, so the dimming rule cannot take it for a
      turn, and styled as neither narration nor an alarm -- nothing went wrong.
      Read `Playthrough::SafetyNotice`'s header before changing a word of it. %>
  <% if safety_notice %>
    <div class="notice">
      <p><strong><%= Playthrough::SafetyNotice::HEADING %></strong></p>
      <% Playthrough::SafetyNotice::PARAGRAPHS.each do |paragraph| %>
        <p><%= paragraph %></p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# THE LINE THE ENGINE WOULD NOT PLAY, and it is NOT a turn: no `Scene` was
      written, story time did not move and the playthrough is standing exactly
      where it was, so there is nothing in the log above for the player to read
      it in. It stands where the turn would have been, in the app's own voice --
      the same place and the same `.notice` as the safety notice, because it is
      the same kind of thing: the app talking rather than the world, and nothing
      has gone wrong.

      The typed line is echoed because the replace takes `#stream` and its `>`
      line away with it, and a refusal with no line above it leaves the player
      guessing which one it answered.

      The copy is `Playthrough::Refusal`'s, engine-authored, out of the records;
      no model wrote a word of it. Read that class's header before changing what
      lands here. %>
  <%# A REFUSAL BECAUSE THE GAME IS OVER SHOWS ONLY THE ECHO. The typed line is
      still worth printing -- it says which line this answered -- but the
      sentence is not, because the standing statement below (a death or a story
      that concluded, whichever the records say) says the same thing in full and
      two of it on one screen reads as a bug.
      `Playthrough::Refusal#game_over?` is the one question that tells the
      shapes apart. %>
  <% if refusal %>
    <p class="command">&gt; <%= refusal.typed %></p>
    <% unless refusal.game_over? %>
      <div class="notice">
        <p><%= refusal.text %></p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# THE GAME IS OVER, AND THIS IS WHERE THE INPUT USED TO BE.

      A playthrough ends for one of two reasons and the copy says which. At zero
      hit points the character is dead (the captain's ruling of 2026-09-04); at
      the last step of the main arc the STORY is over and nobody died
      (`Playthrough::Arc#conclude!`). Either way the form is not disabled and it
      is not left there to be refused line after line -- it is GONE, and what
      stands in its place says why and offers the one thing that is left.

      `Playthrough::EndNotice` DECIDES WHICH, off the records -- an ending row
      or a protagonist at zero -- and this view asks no other question about it.
      It also builds the sentence `Playthrough::Refusal.over` answers a typed
      line with, out of the same two authors (`Playthrough::DeathNotice`,
      `Playthrough::StoryOverNotice`), so the standing statement and the refusal
      cannot come to disagree about why the game is over. Read that class's
      header before changing any of this.

      `#closing_words` is nil on an ordinary finished game and that is the
      point: the ending's own paragraph is the LAST ENTRY OF THE LOG ABOVE, so
      reprinting it here would put it on the screen twice -- the same rule the
      refusal above is under.

      The same `.notice` as the safety notice and the refusal above, and for the
      same reason: this is the app talking rather than the world. It is NOT an
      alert -- nothing went wrong, the game finished. %>
  <% if playthrough.over? %>
    <% ended = Playthrough::EndNotice.for(playthrough) %>
    <div class="notice">
      <p><strong><%= ended.heading %></strong></p>
      <% if ended.closing_words %>
        <p><%= ended.closing_words %></p>
      <% end %>
      <% ended.paragraphs.each do |paragraph| %>
        <p><%= paragraph %></p>
      <% end %>
      <%# The same POST the index's Play button makes, so there is exactly one
          way a playthrough is created and this is not a second one. %>
      <%= form_with url: playthroughs_path do |form| %>
        <%= form.hidden_field :story_id, value: playthrough.story_id %>
        <%= form.submit "Start a new playthrough" %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% elsif command %>
    <p class="command">&gt; <%= command %></p>
    <%# NarrationJob appends batched chunks of prose here, and replaces this
        whole element when the turn lands. The cursor is what the player watches
        during the four to six seconds of prompt processing before the first
        token, and during a move, which cannot stream at all. %>
    <div class="turn cursor" id="stream"></div>
  <% else %>
    <%# A FIGHT, WHEN THE RECORDS SAY THERE IS ONE. Derived and never stored:
        `Playthrough::Battle#on?` is `Playthrough#foes_in` over the room the
        party is standing in, so the panel arrives the turn somebody hostile is
        in front of them and is gone the turn the last one dies or they walk
        out -- with nothing to reconcile either way, because the fight wrote
        through the same records the prose loop writes through.

        Built here rather than in the partial so a game that is not in a fight
        pays one `#foes_in` and no render at all, and inside the `else` on
        purpose: a turn in flight has no form and a game that is over has an
        end notice where all of this would be.

        The buttons post to the same `turns#create` the box below does. See
        `Playthrough::Battle` for the one-UI argument and for what a round
        costs. %>
    <% battle = Playthrough::Battle.new(playthrough) %>
    <% if battle.on? %>
      <%= render "playthroughs/battle", battle: battle, playthrough: playthrough %>
    <% end %>

    <%# Where the player is and what leads out of it. Not decoration: the exits
        are the move targets Playthrough::Classifier will accept, so a player
        who cannot read them is guessing at the one command that matters.

        THE ROOM'S NAME, FOLDED INTO THE PLACE IT IS IN, which is the captain's
        ruling of 2026-09-06: *"Option B."* Rooms have parents since slice 2
        (`Location#containing_place`), so there were two ways to write this line
        -- "in The Rusted Anchor" or "in the taproom of The Rusted Anchor" -- and
        he took the second. So the player reads one sentence -- "You are in the
        taproom of The Rusted Anchor." -- and the room keeps its STORED NAME
        AS-IS, ordinal and all. A room inside nothing keeps the plain sentence
        it has always had.

        THE ARTICLE IS IN THE NAME AND NOT IN THIS LINE, which is what lets one
        sentence read correctly either side of a room being written. An
        unwritten room is called `The Custom House room 1`
        (`Location::Interior.placeholder_name`) and a written one is called
        `the counting room` -- both already carry whatever article English
        wants, so `Location::RoomName` asks the model for one and this prints
        the name it accepted. Adding a "the" here would read "the The Custom
        House room 1" for every room nobody has walked into yet.

        THAT IS THE HALF OF THE RULING THIS FILE OWNS -- the display. The rest
        of it is already true of the records and is nothing for this line to do:
        a room's way out of its place is an ordinary `LocationConnection` to
        that other Location, so the exits below and `Playthrough::Classifier`'s
        closed set name it by THAT PLACE's name already (The Custom House room 1
        lists "The Quay", which `a-building-with-two-floors.yml` walks); and
        naming the place is not required of the player to move between rooms of
        it, because `Playthrough::Grammar::HOW_A_NAME_MATCHES` matches a typed
        fragment by inclusion, so "room 2" resolves from inside the building. %>
    <p class="place">
      <% if playthrough.current_location %>
        <% exits = playthrough.exits %>
        <% place = playthrough.current_location.containing_place %>
        You are in <strong><%= playthrough.current_location.name %></strong><%=
          " of #{place.name}" if place %>.
        Ways out: <%= exits.any? ? exits.map(&:name).to_sentence : "none that you can see." %>
      <% end %>
    </p>

    <%# THE SLASH MENU'S SCOPE, and it is INSIDE `#turn_log` on purpose -- the
        opposite of the play controller's, which has to survive the end-of-turn
        replace. This one has to be RENEWED by it: the closed sets it completes
        against are this turn's exits, cast and items, so the controller is torn
        down and rebuilt with them and there is never a request to make.

        `Playthrough::SlashMenu` is the one author of what is in here, out of
        `Playthrough::Grammar::RESOLVING` and `Playthrough::Classifier#offered_for`
        -- the same closed sets the model is offered and a typed name is matched
        against, so the box cannot offer a word or a name the engine will not
        read back. `/take slate` submits as ordinary text; the slash is stripped
        by `Playthrough::Grammar` before the line is read.

        With no JavaScript this is a plain text field in a plain form and
        nothing is lost but the menu. %>
    <%# THE LINE THE PLAYER JUST SENT, and the reason it is here at all: the
        acknowledgement carries no page (see `TurnsController#create`), and the
        pending page is broadcast by the job only once it owns the game's lock.
        While an earlier turn is still narrating that is the whole of the next
        turn's wait, and before this the page did not change at all -- the typed
        line sat in the box with nothing to say it had been taken, which is
        exactly what makes a player retype. Filled in by `play.js` on a
        successful submit and never by a response, so it cannot land after the
        turn it belongs to; empty on every render, so every broadcast clears it.

        `.command` is the same echo the log and a refusal use. It says the line
        was accepted and claims nothing about what happens next. %>
    <p class="command" data-play-target="receipt" hidden></p>

    <div class="ask" data-controller="slash"
         data-slash-menu-value="<%= Playthrough::SlashMenu.new(playthrough).to_json %>">
      <%= form_with url: playthrough_turns_path(playthrough) do |form| %>
        <%= render "turns/request_token", token: SecureRandom.uuid %>
        <%# Two targets on one field, and they belong to two controllers: `play`
            puts focus back here after a turn (see the note above), `slash`
            reads and completes what is in it. Targets are resolved by a live
            `querySelectorAll` at access time, not from a registry an observer
            fills in later, so this is safe to read in the same tick the stream
            render finished. %>
        <%= form.text_field :command, autofocus: autofocus, autocomplete: "off",
                                      placeholder: "what do you do? (/ to complete)",
                                      role: "combobox", aria: { expanded: false, controls: "slash_menu",
                                                                autocomplete: "list" },
                                      data: { play_target: "command", slash_target: "input",
                                              action: "input->slash#refresh focus->slash#refresh " \
                                                      "keydown->slash#navigate blur->slash#dismiss" } %>
        <%= form.submit "Go" %>
      <% end %>
      <ul class="slash-menu" id="slash_menu" role="listbox" aria-label="commands" data-slash-target="menu" hidden></ul>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
